# Preserving joint references within the compiler budget

Document ID: `reiyah.reference-sharing.checkpoint`. Version: `0.1.0`.

The [bounded change and proof](../research/perception-reference-sharing/0.1.0/README.md)
preserve world-invariant source objects once in the compiled graph while retaining every
joint world and provenance row. A synthetic 17-world comparison now reaches [1/2,3/2]
instead of the earlier conservative [-3/2,3/2]. The source assumptions are unchanged.
The conventional direct-coordinate matching calculation reaches the same exact finite result.

An adversarial larger case rejected unrestricted sharing: a downstream resource fallback
widened [0,1] to [-1,1]. The final preflight uses a conservative bound on the existing core
work formula and declines sharing there. The original compiled input, compilation receipt
and checked packet are recovered byte for byte. Kernel, checker, schemas and limits stay
unchanged. This checkpoint claims two demonstrated precision improvements and a guarded
regression, not universal superiority over ordinary compilation or an analyst.

Read [verification](../research/perception-reference-sharing/0.1.0/verification.json) for exact
sources, measurements, current tests and preserved failures. Common projection still checks
the full inverse structure, including objects whose removal cancels in the loss difference.
Source mapping lookup must use anchor, world and object; a graph ID alone cannot select a
unique original record. Both analysts must retain all mappings and the same joint model.

All supplied cases are synthetic. The ready development viewer exercise remains separate
from independent discovery and actual human reference judgments. No interaction or reviewer
was fabricated. Desktop capability remains diagnosed; no unchanged bootstrap was retried.
An actual participant return takes priority over more machinery.

